// EXPECT: currently-rejected
// Violates: docs/OWNERSHIP.md R2.b (an `owned` position consumes a place, and
// a value that may yield a place on some paths and not others is refused).
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. This was a LIVE double free in ordinary `owned String`
// code, with no `arc` anywhere in it, and it predates every line of the `arc`
// ownership work. Measured end to end against `zig-out/bin/cell` built at
// `0e82266`, with `mk`, `use_it` and `print_int` supplied by a C host:
//
//     cell check                                    exit 0
//     cc -fsanitize=address                         exit 0
//     running it                                    exit 134
//
//     AddressSanitizer: attempting double-free
//       free <- cell_string_free <- cell_main
//
// The emitted C ended:
//
//     cell_string_t s2 = ({ ... _cell_t0 = s1; ... _cell_t0; });
//     cell_print_int(cell_use_it(cell_string_as_str(&s2)));
//     cell_string_free(&s2);
//     cell_string_free(&s1);      /* the same buffer */
//
// WHY IT ESCAPED, which is the part worth keeping. R2 enumerates the forms
// that move a PLACE, and every one of its consumption sites asked `placeOf`
// first: a place moved, and anything else fell through to `checkExpr`, which
// only READS. `placeOf` returns null for a `match`, so `s1` was read rather
// than moved, `wasMoved(s1)` stayed false, and codegen's `pendingDrops` kept
// both `s1` and `s2` because both are droppable, `.owned`, have a drop call
// and are not moved. `emitValueInto`'s leaf then emitted a bitwise
// `_cell_t0 = s1;`, so the two headers hold one buffer.
//
// That is an enumeration of PLACE initializers standing in for a claim about
// every initializer. It is the SAME SHAPE as R10's axis 1, in the general rule
// R10 is a special case of: R10 closed it for `arc` sources and the underlying
// move analysis still had it for every source. The fix is not a longer
// enumeration. `ownedMoveSource` is a TOTAL verdict whose undecidable case is
// `.unknown` and is REFUSED, so a value shape nobody enumerated fails closed.
//
// WHY IT IS REFUSED AND NOT MOVED. A `match` yielding a place from two arms is
// two potential moves of ONE value, and deciding which arm ran is the dataflow
// question `docs/OWNERSHIP.md` 0.3 deliberately does not answer. Reading, which
// is what happened before, is the one answer that is definitely wrong.
//
// SIX consumption sites ask this. Four were live double frees at `0e82266`:
// this `let`, an assignment into an `owned` place, a call argument to an
// `owned` parameter, and a `return`. Two are latent and refused with them: an
// `owned` struct field (a `record` shape is never dropped) and a list-literal
// element (slice elements are never released). Both gaps are R11's.
//
// WHAT STILL COMPILES, so "refused" is scoped to mean something:
//
//     let owned s2: String = s1                        moves, R2 unchanged
//     let owned s2: String = match c { 0 => mk(), _ => mk() }   fresh arms
//     let owned n: Int = match c { 0 => 1, _ => 2 }             scalar arms
//
// The named over-refusal is a `match` over `copy` places in an `owned` slot,
// which was accepted before and is not now. Bind it to a `copy` name first.

pub fn mk() -> String;
pub fn use_it(shared s: String) -> Int;
pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

pub fn main() {
    var copy c = 0
    let owned s1 = mk()
    let owned s2: String = match c { 0 => s1, _ => s1 }
    print_int(use_it(shared s2))
}
